board_atmega4809_oled — modern-AVR SSD1306 OLED over I2C and SPI
===============================================================

A 128x64 SSD1306 OLED driven by an ATmega4809, with an OpenGL window that
renders the panel. It comes in two flavours from one board, to show the modern
AVR's two serial buses carrying a real display protocol:

  * I2C  — the panel on TWI0 (avr_twi_modern), address 0x3C
  * SPI  — the panel on SPI0 (avr_spi_modern), 4-wire mode with CS/DC/RST GPIOs

Both reuse simavr's existing SSD1306 model (examples/parts/ssd1306_virt) and its
OpenGL renderer (ssd1306_glut) — the same parts the classic board_ssd1306 uses.

Build & run (from this folder)
------------------------------

Open a console, change into this folder, build, then run one of the buses:

    cd examples/board_atmega4809_oled    # the host loads its .axf from here
    make                                 # builds both firmwares + the host
    ./obj-*/oled.elf i2c                 # SSD1306 over I2C   (default)
    ./obj-*/oled.elf spi                 # SSD1306 over SPI

A window appears showing a checkerboard with a vertical bar sweeping across it.
Press 'q' or Esc in the window to quit. The runnable program is the host binary
under obj-<arch>/ (e.g. obj-x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/oled.elf); run it from this
folder so it finds atmega4809_oled_<bus>.axf.

No display? Verify it headlessly
--------------------------------

On a machine or CI with no graphics, append "check": the host runs the firmware
for ~300 ms with no window and reports how many panel pixels were lit, proving
the bus carried the image:

    ./obj-*/oled.elf i2c check       # -> PASS  i2c: 4160/8192 pixels lit ...
    ./obj-*/oled.elf spi check       # -> PASS  spi: 4160/8192 pixels lit ...

How it works
------------

  * The firmware (atmega4809_oled_i2c.c / _spi.c) runs the standard SSD1306
    power-on/init sequence, then streams an 8-page x 128-column framebuffer.
    I2C uses the 0x00/0x40 control bytes; SPI uses CS=PC0, DC=PC1, RST=PC2.

  * The host (oled.c) attaches ssd1306_virt and wires it to the bus with the
    shared ssd1306_connect / ssd1306_connect_twi helpers. Those helpers take a
    bus-name argument: classic cores register their SPI/TWI bus under integer
    name 0, while modern (AVRxt) cores use the char name '0', so oled.c passes
    '0'. The AVR runs in a thread throttled to real time so the sweep is
    watchable, and ssd1306_glut draws the vram each frame.

Requirements
------------

OpenGL + GLUT (freeglut) for the window (pulled in via ../Makefile.opengl), and
a modern avr-gcc (12+) with the megaAVR-0 headers for the firmware. If avr-gcc
cannot target atmega4809 the firmware step is skipped (a plain `make` across
examples/ still succeeds) — install a modern avr-gcc and re-run `make`.
